- How would you handle negative values or all-negative arrays?
- How would you adapt to a similar constraint with k-adjacent bans (no more than two chosen in any window of size k)?

## Why This Is Asked
Beginner DP concepts with a new angle: counting unordered combinations (coin change ways) and contrasting memoization with tabulation.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming (top-down memoization)
- Tabulation (bottom-up DP)
- Unordered combinations vs permutations
- Space optimization (1D DP)

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWaysTopDown(coins, target) {
  const n = coins.length;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, rem) {
    if (rem === 0) return 1;
    if (rem < 0 || i === n) return 0;
    const key = i + '|' + rem;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i + 1, rem) + dfs(i, rem - coins[i]);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}

function countWaysBottomUp(coins, target) {
  const dp = new Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const c of coins) {
    for (let r = c; r <= target; r++) {
      dp[r] += dp[r - c];
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you count permutations (order matters) instead of combinations?
- How would you handle very large targets or mod results to avoid overflow in languages with fixed integer sizes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a DP with variable-length segments and per-segment value, plus both memoized and tabulated solutions.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming over positions with limited jump length
- Maintaining per-iteration maximums within a window
- Bottom-up and top-down (memoization) DP variants

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumPartition(arr, k) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    let maxInWin = -Infinity;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k && i + t <= n; t++) {
      maxInWin = Math.max(maxInWin, arr[i + t - 1]);
      dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], maxInWin + dp[i + t]);
    }
  }
  return dp[0];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize the inner max to O(n) using a deque when k is large?
- How would you reconstruct the actual partition that yields the maximum sum?

## Why This Is Asked

Duplicate values are common in real-world data sets. Traditional 2-way quicksort performs poorly with many duplicates, leading to unnecessary comparisons and degraded performance. 3-way partitioning addresses this by grouping equal elements together, significantly reducing the algorithm's work.

## Key Concepts

- **3-way partitioning (Dutch national flag algorithm)**: Separates array into < pivot, == pivot, and > pivot regions
- **Randomized pivot selection**: Prevents worst-case O(n²) scenarios on already sorted or adversarial input
- **In-place sorting**: Operates with O(1) additional space, using only O(log n) expected stack depth
- **Performance optimization**: Reduces comparisons from O(n log n) to O(n) when many duplicates exist

## Code Example

```javascript
function quicksort3(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;

  // Random pivot selection
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random() * (hi - lo + 1)) + lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;

  // 3-way partitioning
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) {
      [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]];
      lt++;
      i++;
    } else if (a[i] > pivot) {
      [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]];
      gt--;
    } else {
      i++; // a[i] === pivot
    }
  }

  // Recursively sort subarrays
  quicksort3(a, lo, lt - 1);
  quicksort3(a, gt + 1, hi);
}
```

## Partitioning Strategy

The algorithm maintains three regions during partitioning:
- `[lo, lt-1]`: elements < pivot
- `[lt, gt]`: elements === pivot (being processed)
- `[gt+1, hi]`: elements > pivot

As we iterate, elements are swapped into their appropriate region, with equal elements naturally forming the middle section that needs no further processing.

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of sorting trade-offs (in-place memory vs stability vs constants) and how hybrid approaches balance speed, especially relevant in finance and large-scale data.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning in quicksort
- Insertion sort for small slices
- In-place sorting and O(1) extra space
- Average vs worst-case complexities; pivot choice

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortHybrid(a){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(i,j)=>{ [a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]]; };
  const insertSort=(l,r)=>{
    for(let i=l+1;i<=r;i++){
      let x=a[i], j=i-1;
      while(j>=l && a[j]>x){ a[j+1]=a[j]; j--; }
      a[j+1]=x;
    }
  };
  const partition=(lo,hi)=>{
    const p=a[lo+((hi-lo)>>1)];
    let i=lo, lt=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(a[i]<p){ swap(i,lt); i++; lt++; }
      else if(a[i]>p){ swap(i,gt); gt--; }
      else i++;
    }
    return [lt,gt];
  };
  const qs=(lo,hi)=>{
    if(lo>=hi) return;
    if(hi-lo+1 <= TH){ insertSort(lo,hi); return; }
    const [l,r] = partition(lo,hi);
    qs(lo, l-1);
    qs(r+1, hi);
  };
  qs(0,a.length-1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design streaming state with sliding windows, using hash maps, per-id deques (linked lists), and a heap for ranking. Emphasizes correctness under out-of-order arrivals and lazy expirations.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding-window maintenance per key using a deque
- Lazy expiration and lazy heap invalidation
- Top-K extraction via a max-heap with versioning

## Code Example
```javascript
class RealTimeTopKWindow {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.byId = new Map(); // id -> { deque: [], sum, version }
    this.globalMax = new MinHeap((a,b)=>b.sum-a.sum); // max-heap emulation
  }
  addEvent(id, value, ts) {
    // Ensure id exists
    const rec = this.byId.get(id) || { deque: [], sum: 0, version: 0 };
    // expire old
    while (rec.deque.length && rec.deque[0].ts <= ts - this.W) {
      const old = rec.deque.shift();
      rec.sum -= old.value;
    }
    rec.deque.push({ ts, value });
    rec.sum += value;
    rec.version++;
    this.byId.set(id, rec);
    this.globalMax.push({ sum: rec.sum, id, ver: rec.version });
  }
  getTopK(K) {
    const res = [];
    const seen = new Set();
    while (res.length < K) {
      const top = this.globalMax.pop();
      if (!top) break;
      const cur = this.byId.get(top.id);
      if (!cur || cur.version !== top.ver || top.sum !== cur.sum) continue; // stale
      if (!seen.has(top.id)) {
        res.push(top.id);
        seen.add(top.id);
      }
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if W varies by id? 
- How to scale to millions of ids with bounded memory?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in per-prefix top-K maintenance using a Trie with bounded heaps and dynamic frequency updates, revealing trade-offs between speed and memory.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node top-K storage using a min-heap with bounded size K
- Min-heap with tie-breaking by word for stable ordering
- Dynamic frequency updates with addWord(word, delta) supporting negative deltas
- Automatic deletion when frequency reaches zero

## Time and Memory Trade-offs
- **Time Complexity**: addWord runs in O(L × log K) where L is word length, as we traverse the path and perform heap operations. getTopK runs in O(K × log K) for heap extraction plus O(K log K) for sorting.
- **Space Complexity**: O(N × L + N × K) where N is the total number of unique words—the Trie itself costs O(N × L), and each node stores up to K heap entries.
- **Trade-off**: Increasing K improves result quality but increases memory usage and slows updates. A smaller K is faster but returns fewer suggestions.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.topK = new MinHeap((a, b) => {
      if (a.freq !== b.freq) return a.freq - b.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });
  }
}

class AutocompleteEngine {
  constructor(K = 3) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.freqMap = new Map();
    this.K = K;
  }

  addWord(word, delta) {
    const newFreq = (this.freqMap.get(word) || 0) + delta;
    if (newFreq <= 0) {
      this.freqMap.delete(word);
    } else {
      this.freqMap.set(word, newFreq);
    }

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.topK.upsert({ word, freq: newFreq }, this.K);
    }
  }

  getTopK(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTop(K);
  }
}
```

